$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 05/Practice
::HC-W5-P01::A district presents a completed sanitation plan at a village meeting and calls the meeting co-design. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=one-way information presented as participation#Correct. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
~representation gap#Not the best answer. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
~incomplete feedback loop#Not the best answer. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
~failure to investigate a community signal#Not the best answer. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
}

::HC-W5-P02::A district presents a completed sanitation plan at a village meeting and calls the meeting co-design. What is the strongest next response? {
=Describe the activity honestly as information sharing and create a stage where community input can still change decisions.#Correct. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
~Create safe, separate and accessible ways for adolescent girls to contribute to decisions.#Not the best answer. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
~Analyse concerns, assign action, and communicate back what changed or why no change was possible.#Not the best answer. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
~Check technical dosing and access conditions, then explain findings transparently with community participation.#Not the best answer. Co-design requires influence over design, not only attendance.
}

::HC-W5-P03::Community meetings are attended mainly by male elders while adolescent girls are the primary users of the service being redesigned. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=representation gap#Correct. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
~incomplete feedback loop#Not the best answer. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
~failure to investigate a community signal#Not the best answer. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
~elite capture risk#Not the best answer. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
}

::HC-W5-P04::Community meetings are attended mainly by male elders while adolescent girls are the primary users of the service being redesigned. What is the strongest next response? {
=Create safe, separate and accessible ways for adolescent girls to contribute to decisions.#Correct. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
~Analyse concerns, assign action, and communicate back what changed or why no change was possible.#Not the best answer. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
~Check technical dosing and access conditions, then explain findings transparently with community participation.#Not the best answer. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
~Map who is affected and deliberately include less powerful or less visible groups.#Not the best answer. Formal leadership does not represent every affected group.
}

::HC-W5-P05::A hotline collects hundreds of complaints but callers never hear whether anything changed. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=incomplete feedback loop#Correct. Listening without closure can erode trust.
~failure to investigate a community signal#Not the best answer. Listening without closure can erode trust.
~elite capture risk#Not the best answer. Listening without closure can erode trust.
~unrealistic commitment#Not the best answer. Listening without closure can erode trust.
}

::HC-W5-P06::A hotline collects hundreds of complaints but callers never hear whether anything changed. What is the strongest next response? {
=Analyse concerns, assign action, and communicate back what changed or why no change was possible.#Correct. Listening without closure can erode trust.
~Check technical dosing and access conditions, then explain findings transparently with community participation.#Not the best answer. Listening without closure can erode trust.
~Map who is affected and deliberately include less powerful or less visible groups.#Not the best answer. Listening without closure can erode trust.
~State what is and is not within the team’s authority and avoid promises that cannot be guaranteed.#Not the best answer. Listening without closure can erode trust.
}

::HC-W5-P07::Residents report that chlorinated water tastes strange, and officials dismiss the concern as ignorance. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=failure to investigate a community signal#Correct. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
~elite capture risk#Not the best answer. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
~unrealistic commitment#Not the best answer. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
~misuse of qualitative signal data#Not the best answer. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
}

::HC-W5-P08::Residents report that chlorinated water tastes strange, and officials dismiss the concern as ignorance. What is the strongest next response? {
=Check technical dosing and access conditions, then explain findings transparently with community participation.#Correct. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
~Map who is affected and deliberately include less powerful or less visible groups.#Not the best answer. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
~State what is and is not within the team’s authority and avoid promises that cannot be guaranteed.#Not the best answer. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
~Treat reports as signals, code and triangulate them, and avoid population prevalence claims without representative data.#Not the best answer. A concern may reveal a real operational issue as well as a communication need.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 05/Mastery
::HC-W5-M01::A programme invites the same leaders to every consultation because they are easy to reach. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=elite capture risk#Correct. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
~unrealistic commitment#Not the best answer. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
~misuse of qualitative signal data#Not the best answer. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
~unsafe engagement design#Not the best answer. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
}

::HC-W5-M02::A programme invites the same leaders to every consultation because they are easy to reach. What is the strongest next response? {
=Map who is affected and deliberately include less powerful or less visible groups.#Correct. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
~State what is and is not within the team’s authority and avoid promises that cannot be guaranteed.#Not the best answer. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
~Treat reports as signals, code and triangulate them, and avoid population prevalence claims without representative data.#Not the best answer. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
~Use confidential and trauma-informed channels that do not expose participants.#Not the best answer. Convenient participation can reproduce existing power inequalities.
}

::HC-W5-M03::A refugee-health team promises a new clinic during consultation before funding is approved. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unrealistic commitment#Correct. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
~misuse of qualitative signal data#Not the best answer. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
~unsafe engagement design#Not the best answer. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
~closed feedback loop with adaptation#Not the best answer. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
}

::HC-W5-M04::A refugee-health team promises a new clinic during consultation before funding is approved. What is the strongest next response? {
=State what is and is not within the team’s authority and avoid promises that cannot be guaranteed.#Correct. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
~Treat reports as signals, code and triangulate them, and avoid population prevalence claims without representative data.#Not the best answer. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
~Use confidential and trauma-informed channels that do not expose participants.#Not the best answer. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
~Document the concern, adaptation and subsequent use to assess whether the change improved access.#Not the best answer. Engagement creates expectations and requires honesty.
}

::HC-W5-M05::Rumours from community health workers are entered into a spreadsheet and reported as the percentage of residents who believe them. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=misuse of qualitative signal data#Correct. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
~unsafe engagement design#Not the best answer. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
~closed feedback loop with adaptation#Not the best answer. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
~output mistaken for influence#Not the best answer. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
}

::HC-W5-M06::Rumours from community health workers are entered into a spreadsheet and reported as the percentage of residents who believe them. What is the strongest next response? {
=Treat reports as signals, code and triangulate them, and avoid population prevalence claims without representative data.#Correct. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
~Use confidential and trauma-informed channels that do not expose participants.#Not the best answer. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
~Document the concern, adaptation and subsequent use to assess whether the change improved access.#Not the best answer. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
~Measure whose views were included and whether community input changed priorities or implementation.#Not the best answer. Community listening is valuable but not automatically a survey.
}

::HC-W5-M07::Women avoid a public meeting on gender-based violence because attendance could reveal personal experiences. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unsafe engagement design#Correct. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
~closed feedback loop with adaptation#Not the best answer. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
~output mistaken for influence#Not the best answer. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
~public conformity risk#Not the best answer. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
}

::HC-W5-M08::Women avoid a public meeting on gender-based violence because attendance could reveal personal experiences. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use confidential and trauma-informed channels that do not expose participants.#Correct. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
~Document the concern, adaptation and subsequent use to assess whether the change improved access.#Not the best answer. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
~Measure whose views were included and whether community input changed priorities or implementation.#Not the best answer. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
~Use additional private or subgroup methods to detect dissent and competing views.#Not the best answer. Participation methods can themselves create harm.
}

::HC-W5-M09::A vaccination team changes clinic hours after repeated community feedback and tells residents why the schedule changed. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=closed feedback loop with adaptation#Correct. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
~output mistaken for influence#Not the best answer. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
~public conformity risk#Not the best answer. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
~participation fatigue and extractive consultation#Not the best answer. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
}

::HC-W5-M10::A vaccination team changes clinic hours after repeated community feedback and tells residents why the schedule changed. What is the strongest next response? {
=Document the concern, adaptation and subsequent use to assess whether the change improved access.#Correct. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
~Measure whose views were included and whether community input changed priorities or implementation.#Not the best answer. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
~Use additional private or subgroup methods to detect dissent and competing views.#Not the best answer. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
~Reduce repetitive consultation, clarify decision scope and report back consistently.#Not the best answer. Engagement is strongest when feedback visibly influences operations.
}

::HC-W5-M11::Attendance at community dialogues doubles, and the programme concludes community ownership doubled. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=output mistaken for influence#Correct. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
~public conformity risk#Not the best answer. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
~participation fatigue and extractive consultation#Not the best answer. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
~unnecessary data collection#Not the best answer. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
}

::HC-W5-M12::Attendance at community dialogues doubles, and the programme concludes community ownership doubled. What is the strongest next response? {
=Measure whose views were included and whether community input changed priorities or implementation.#Correct. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
~Use additional private or subgroup methods to detect dissent and competing views.#Not the best answer. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
~Reduce repetitive consultation, clarify decision scope and report back consistently.#Not the best answer. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
~Minimise identifiers, restrict access and establish retention rules for community feedback.#Not the best answer. Attendance counts participation volume, not decision power.
}

::HC-W5-M13::A local leader publicly agrees with an intervention, and the team records unanimous community support. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=public conformity risk#Correct. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
~participation fatigue and extractive consultation#Not the best answer. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
~unnecessary data collection#Not the best answer. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
~weak attribution#Not the best answer. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
}

::HC-W5-M14::A local leader publicly agrees with an intervention, and the team records unanimous community support. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use additional private or subgroup methods to detect dissent and competing views.#Correct. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
~Reduce repetitive consultation, clarify decision scope and report back consistently.#Not the best answer. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
~Minimise identifiers, restrict access and establish retention rules for community feedback.#Not the best answer. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
~Examine concurrent service changes and use comparison or contribution evidence before assigning causality.#Not the best answer. Public agreement can be shaped by power and social pressure.
}

::HC-W5-M15::A disease-control programme asks communities for ideas every month but rarely implements or responds to suggestions. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=participation fatigue and extractive consultation#Correct. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
~unnecessary data collection#Not the best answer. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
~weak attribution#Not the best answer. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
~conflicting interests within community#Not the best answer. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
}

::HC-W5-M16::A disease-control programme asks communities for ideas every month but rarely implements or responds to suggestions. What is the strongest next response? {
=Reduce repetitive consultation, clarify decision scope and report back consistently.#Correct. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
~Minimise identifiers, restrict access and establish retention rules for community feedback.#Not the best answer. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
~Examine concurrent service changes and use comparison or contribution evidence before assigning causality.#Not the best answer. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
~Make trade-offs explicit, use transparent criteria and document whose needs are prioritised and why.#Not the best answer. Repeated listening without response can reduce willingness to engage.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 05/Institutional
::HC-W5-I01::A feedback dataset stores names, phone numbers and sensitive complaints even though identifiers are not needed for analysis. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unnecessary data collection#Correct. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
~weak attribution#Not the best answer. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
~conflicting interests within community#Not the best answer. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
~one-way information presented as participation#Not the best answer. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
}

::HC-W5-I02::A feedback dataset stores names, phone numbers and sensitive complaints even though identifiers are not needed for analysis. What is the strongest next response? {
=Minimise identifiers, restrict access and establish retention rules for community feedback.#Correct. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
~Examine concurrent service changes and use comparison or contribution evidence before assigning causality.#Not the best answer. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
~Make trade-offs explicit, use transparent criteria and document whose needs are prioritised and why.#Not the best answer. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
~Describe the activity honestly as information sharing and create a stage where community input can still change decisions.#Not the best answer. Accountability data still require privacy protection.
}

::HC-W5-I03::An NGO concludes a participatory approach caused higher service uptake because uptake rose after community meetings. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=weak attribution#Correct. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
~conflicting interests within community#Not the best answer. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
~one-way information presented as participation#Not the best answer. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
~representation gap#Not the best answer. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
}

::HC-W5-I04::An NGO concludes a participatory approach caused higher service uptake because uptake rose after community meetings. What is the strongest next response? {
=Examine concurrent service changes and use comparison or contribution evidence before assigning causality.#Correct. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
~Make trade-offs explicit, use transparent criteria and document whose needs are prioritised and why.#Not the best answer. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
~Describe the activity honestly as information sharing and create a stage where community input can still change decisions.#Not the best answer. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
~Create safe, separate and accessible ways for adolescent girls to contribute to decisions.#Not the best answer. Before-after change alone cannot isolate the effect of engagement.
}

::HC-W5-I05::Two community groups disagree sharply about where a new service point should be located. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=conflicting interests within community#Correct. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
~one-way information presented as participation#Not the best answer. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
~representation gap#Not the best answer. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
~incomplete feedback loop#Not the best answer. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
}

::HC-W5-I06::Two community groups disagree sharply about where a new service point should be located. What is the strongest next response? {
=Make trade-offs explicit, use transparent criteria and document whose needs are prioritised and why.#Correct. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
~Describe the activity honestly as information sharing and create a stage where community input can still change decisions.#Not the best answer. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
~Create safe, separate and accessible ways for adolescent girls to contribute to decisions.#Not the best answer. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
~Analyse concerns, assign action, and communicate back what changed or why no change was possible.#Not the best answer. Communities are not homogeneous and engagement does not guarantee consensus.
